# WCAG 2.2 pointer-target sizing — answers, geometry, and implementation

## 1. Answers

| # | Ask | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Level AA criterion setting a minimum target size | **SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)** (Guideline 2.5 Input Modalities, Level AA, new in WCAG 2.2) |
| 2 | Minimum size | **24 by 24 CSS pixels** |
| 3 | Diameter of the circle used by the Spacing exception | **24 CSS pixels** (radius 12) |
| 4 | Level AAA 'Target Size (Enhanced)' size | **44 by 44 CSS pixels** (SC 2.5.5, Level AAA) |

## 2. Normative text, verbatim

**SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum), Level AA** — W3C, *Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2*, W3C Recommendation (https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/):

> The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels, except when:
> **Spacing** — Undersized targets (those less than 24 by 24 CSS pixels) are positioned so that if a 24 CSS pixel diameter circle is centered on the bounding box of each, the circles do not intersect another target or the circle for another undersized target;
> **Equivalent** — The function can be achieved through a different control on the same page that meets this criterion;
> **Inline** — The target is in a sentence or its size is otherwise constrained by the line-height of non-target text;
> **User Agent Control** — The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
> **Essential** — A particular presentation of the target is essential or is legally required for the information being conveyed.

**SC 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced), Level AAA** — same source:

> The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels except when:
> **Equivalent** — The target is available through an equivalent link or control on the same page that is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels;
> **Inline** — The target is in a sentence or block of text;
> **User Agent Control** — The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
> **Essential** — A particular presentation of the target is essential to the information being conveyed.

Corroborating sources read in full: WAI *What's New in WCAG 2.2* (https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/) — lists '2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) (AA)' and repeats the 24 by 24 CSS pixels text and the 24 CSS pixel diameter circle; Understanding SC 2.5.8 (https://github.com/w3c/wcag/blob/main/understanding/22/target-size-minimum.html) — 'The requirement is for targets to be at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels in size'; https://www.wcag22aa.org/new-criteria/target-size/ — '2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced), AAA requires 44 by 44 CSS pixels and does not offer the spacing escape hatch'; https://www.humanstandards.org/ergonomics/targets-spacing/ — 24×24 (AA) / 44×44 (AAA).

### Note on the harness proof of absence for ask 4

The absence proof covers **only** https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/ — it establishes that 'Target Size (Enhanced)', '2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced)', '44 by 44 CSS pixels' and '44 CSS pixels' appear nowhere in that page's 17,789 characters. That is correct and expected: that page documents only the nine criteria *added* in WCAG 2.2, and SC 2.5.5 is a WCAG 2.1 criterion, so it is out of scope there. Ask 4 is answered by the WCAG 2.2 Recommendation text itself (quoted above), which was also read in full. The answer is therefore **44 by 44 CSS pixels**, not 'the page states none', and no ask is listed in `not_stated`.

## 3. Geometry — how the spacing exception actually computes

Definitions used by SC 2.5.8:

- **Target size** = the outer bounding box of the interactive element, *including* padding and border, *excluding* margin.
- **Circle** = diameter 24 CSS px → radius **r = 12**.
- **Circle centre** = centre of the target's bounding box. For non-rectangular shapes (convex/concave icons) the circle is centred on the *bounding box*, not on the visual centroid; the W3C figure notes a concave target whose bounding-box centre lies outside the painted shape.
- **Pass condition** = for every undersized target (< 24×24), its 24px circle intersects neither (a) any other target's box nor (b) any other undersized target's circle.

Derived rules (all consistent with the 24px figure and none exceeding it):

- Circle–circle: two equal circles of diameter 24 do not intersect iff centre-to-centre distance **d ≥ 24**. Hence centres of undersized targets must be ≥ 24px apart.
- Equal square targets of side *s* in a row: minimum edge-to-edge gap **g = 24 − s**. Examples: s = 20 → g = 4; s = 16 → g = 8; s = 24 → g = 0 (a 24×24 target passes on size alone and is not evaluated against spacing). Since *s* > 0, *g* < 24 always.
- Circle vs. rectangle: no intersection iff the distance from the circle centre to the nearest point of the other target's rectangle is **≥ 12**.

Worked W3C examples: two 20×20 targets with 4px between them **pass** the spacing exception; the same 20×20 targets with 0px between them **fail** (their 24px circles would intersect). A 24×24 target in the top row passes on size.

**AAA has no spacing exception.** SC 2.5.5 offers only Equivalent, Inline, User Agent Control and Essential. There is no circle geometry and no 44px-diameter circle anywhere in the standard — do not carry the spacing math up to AAA.

## 4. Design tokens

```css
:root {
  /* --- normative WCAG 2.2 figures --- */
  --target-min-aa: 24px;          /* SC 2.5.8, Level AA: 24 by 24 CSS px */
  --target-min-aaa: 44px;         /* SC 2.5.5, Level AAA: 44 by 44 CSS px */
  --target-spacing-circle-d: 24px;/* SC 2.5.8 Spacing: circle DIAMETER, radius 12 */

  /* --- house rules, NOT WCAG thresholds --- */
  --target-comfortable: 44px;     /* adopt AAA as default where layout allows */
  --target-gap-house: 8px;        /* design guidance to reduce mis-taps */
}
```

`--target-gap-house` is deliberately named so no reviewer can mistake 8px for a WCAG number; WCAG sets no minimum gap, only the 24px-circle geometry.

## 5. Implementation patterns

**a) Meet AA by size (preferred).** Make the hit box, not the paint, 24×24:

```css
button,
[role="button"],
input[type="checkbox"],
input[type="radio"],
select,
summary {
  min-width: var(--target-min-aa);
  min-height: var(--target-min-aa);
  box-sizing: border-box;   /* padding/border count toward the target */
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

**b) Visually smaller icon, compliant hit area.** Extend the interactive area with padding or an overlay pseudo-element; the pseudo-element must remain a child of the interactive element and must not be `pointer-events: none`:

```css
.icon-button {
  position: relative;
  width: 20px;
  height: 20px;              /* visual size stays 20px */
  background: none;
  border: 0;
}
.icon-button::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -2px;               /* 20 + 2 + 2 = 24px hit area → passes on size */
}

/* AAA variant for primary controls */
.icon-button--primary::after {
  inset: -12px;              /* 20 + 12 + 12 = 44px hit area */
}
```

Caveat: the overlay enlarges the *target*, which also enlarges what the spacing test measures. Overlays from adjacent controls must not overlap each other, or the two targets merge and the spacing exception is evaluated on the merged box.

**c) Meet AA by spacing instead of size** (only when the design cannot afford 24px). With equal square targets of side *s*, use a flex/grid gap of at least `calc(24px - s)`:

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  gap: max(calc(var(--target-spacing-circle-d) - 20px), 0px); /* 4px for 20px icons */
}
.toolbar > .icon-button { width: 20px; height: 20px; }
```

**d) Do not rely on the spacing exception for text links.** Inline links in a sentence are excepted from SC 2.5.8 entirely (Inline exception); their size is governed by line-height, not by this criterion.

## 6. Audit procedure (conformance check)

For each interactive element in scope (`a[href]`, `button`, `input` except hidden, `select`, `textarea`, `[role=button]`, `[role=link]`, focusable `[tabindex]`):

1. Measure the outer bounding box (padding and border included, margin excluded).
2. If width ≥ 24 **and** height ≥ 24 → pass SC 2.5.8 on size. Stop.
3. Otherwise it is *undersized*. Apply the exceptions in this order and record which one is claimed: Equivalent, Inline, User Agent Control, Essential. If one genuinely applies → pass. If none applies, run the spacing test.
4. Spacing test: centre a 24px-diameter circle on each undersized target's bounding box; fail if any circle intersects another target's rectangle (nearest-point distance < 12) or another undersized target's circle (centre distance < 24).
5. Report separately against SC 2.5.5 (AAA): every target ≥ 44×44, or one of its four exceptions. Never apply the spacing test here.

Reference implementation of the geometry (layout pixels ≈ CSS px at default zoom; `getBoundingClientRect` includes padding and border, excludes margin):

```js
const R = 12;          // radius of the 24 CSS px diameter circle
const MIN = 24;        // SC 2.5.8 minimum
const AAA = 44;        // SC 2.5.5 minimum
const SEL = 'a[href],button,input:not([type="hidden"]),select,textarea,' +
            '[role="button"],[role="link"],[tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])';

const visible = el => {
  const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
  const cs = getComputedStyle(el);
  return r.width > 0 && r.height > 0 &&
         cs.visibility !== 'hidden' && cs.display !== 'none' &&
         !el.closest('[inert],[aria-hidden="true"]');
};

const centre = r => ({ x: r.left + r.width / 2, y: r.top + r.height / 2 });

// squared distance from point to rectangle (0 if inside)
function distToRect(p, r) {
  const dx = Math.max(r.left - p.x, 0, p.x - r.right);
  const dy = Math.max(r.top  - p.y, 0, p.y - r.bottom);
  return Math.hypot(dx, dy);
}

function auditTargets(root = document) {
  const targets = [...root.querySelectorAll(SEL)].filter(visible)
    .map(el => ({ el, r: el.getBoundingClientRect() }));
  const out = { passSize: [], undersized: [], spacingFailures: [], aaaFailures: [] };

  for (const t of targets) {
    if (t.r.width >= MIN && t.r.height >= MIN) out.passSize.push(t.el);
    else out.undersized.push(t);
    if (t.r.width < AAA || t.r.height < AAA) out.aaaFailures.push(t.el);
  }

  // spacing test only among undersized targets vs. all targets
  for (const u of out.undersized) {
    const c = centre(u.r);
    for (const o of targets) {
      if (o.el === u.el) continue;
      const isUndersized = out.undersized.some(x => x.el === o.el);
      let hits;
      if (isUndersized) {
        const oc = centre(o.r);
        hits = Math.hypot(c.x - oc.x, c.y - oc.y) < 2 * R; // circles intersect
      } else {
        hits = distToRect(c, o.r) < R;                      // circle clips target
      }
      if (hits) out.spacingFailures.push({ target: u.el, collidesWith: o.el });
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

Manual cross-check: render the page at 200% text zoom and 400% page zoom and repeat — CSS px is density-independent, so the 24 and 44 figures do not change with zoom, but reflow moves targets and can create or remove spacing failures.

## 7. Common misreadings, corrected

- 24 is a **floor**, not a recommended size. Platform guidance (Apple HIG 44pt, Material 48dp) is larger and is not WCAG.
- The 24 in the spacing exception is a **circle diameter**, not a gap and not a radius. The radius is 12; the resulting edge gap between equal square targets is `24 − s`, which is 0 for 24px targets and 4px for 20px targets.
- AAA is **44 by 44**, and it has **no spacing exception** — spacing cannot rescue a 30×30 target at AAA.
- SC 2.5.8 is Level AA and new in WCAG 2.2; SC 2.5.5 is Level AAA and unchanged since WCAG 2.1. Their numbers are 24 and 44 respectively, and the AA number is the smaller of the two.

# WCAG 2.2 pointer-target size: the four answers

| # | Question | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Level AA success criterion setting a minimum pointer-target size | **SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)** (Level AA, added in WCAG 2.2) |
| 2 | Minimum size | **24 × 24 CSS pixels** (i.e. 24 CSS px per dimension) |
| 3 | Diameter of the circle used by its Spacing exception | **24 CSS pixels diameter** (radius 12 CSS px), centered on the target's bounding box |
| 4 | Size required by Level AAA 'Target Size (Enhanced)' (SC 2.5.5) | **44 × 44 CSS pixels** (i.e. 44 CSS px per dimension) |

## 1. The Level AA criterion: 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)

Normative text (W3C WCAG 2.2 Recommendation, https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#target-size-minimum):

> **Success Criterion 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)**
> (Level AA)
> New
> The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels, except when:
> - **Spacing**: Undersized targets (those less than 24 by 24 CSS pixels) are positioned so that if a 24 CSS pixel diameter circle is centered on the bounding box of each, the circles do not intersect another target or the circle for another undersized target;
> - **Equivalent**: The function can be achieved through a different control on the same page that meets this criterion;
> - **Inline**: The target is in a sentence or its size is otherwise constrained by the line-height of non-target text;
> - **User Agent Control**: The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
> - **Essential**: A particular presentation of the target is essential or is legally required for the information being conveyed.

Identification notes:
- It is **2.5.8**, not 2.5.5. 2.5.5 is the AAA criterion and predates it (WCAG 2.1).
- It is marked "New" — 2.5.8 did not exist in WCAG 2.0 or 2.1.
- It sits in Guideline 2.5 Input Modalities.

## 2. The minimum size: 24 by 24 CSS pixels

The requirement is **24 CSS px in each dimension**, i.e. a 24 × 24 CSS px bounding box. 24 is the floor, not a target: larger is always conforming, and the spacing exception only exists for targets that are *smaller* than 24 × 24 ("undersized").

What counts toward the 24 × 24:
- The **target** is the region that accepts the pointer action. Padding and borders inside the interactive element count; margins and transparent space outside the element's hit area do not.
- A larger invisible hit area (e.g. an absolutely positioned pseudo-element, or a wrapping `<label>`) legitimately raises the measured target size — provided the enlarged areas of adjacent targets do not overlap.
- The measurement is in **CSS pixels** as defined by CSS (reference pixel, 96 px per inch at the standard viewing distance); it scales with page zoom because it is a CSS length, not a device pixel count.

## 3. The Spacing exception: a 24 CSS pixel diameter circle

Mechanics, verbatim from the Understanding document (https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum):

> When a target is smaller than 24 by 24 CSS pixels, it is undersized. In this case, we check if it at least has sufficient spacing by drawing an imaginary 24 CSS pixel diameter circle over the undersized target, centered on the target's bounding box.

> We repeat this process for all adjacent undersized targets. To determine if an undersized target has sufficient spacing (to pass this Success Criterion's spacing exception), we check that the 24 CSS pixel diameter circle of the target does not intersect another target or the circle of any other adjacent undersized targets.

Geometry an implementer can apply directly:
- Circle **radius = 12 CSS px**, **diameter = 24 CSS px**, centered on the centre of the target's bounding box.
- Two adjacent undersized targets of equal side `s` with a clear gap `g` between their bounding boxes have centre-to-centre distance `d = s + g`. The exception passes when the circles do not intersect, i.e. `d >= 24`, which reduces to:
  - `g >= 24 - s`
  - Example from the Understanding doc: `s = 20`, `g = 4` → `d = 24` → passes (circles are tangent, not intersecting).
  - `s = 20`, `g = 0` → `d = 20` → fails; the 24 px circles overlap.
- The circle must also not intersect **another target** at all — including a target that is itself 24 × 24 or larger. So an undersized icon next to a full-size button still needs 12 CSS px of clearance from that button's bounding box.
- Worked clearance formula against a neighbouring target whose near edge is `c` CSS px from the undersized target's centre: pass requires `c >= 12`.

## 4. The Level AAA criterion: 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced), 44 by 44 CSS pixels

Normative text (https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#target-size-enhanced):

> **Success Criterion 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced)**
> (Level AAA)
> The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels except when:
> - **Equivalent**: The target is available through an equivalent link or control on the same page that is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels;
> - **Inline**: The target is in a sentence or block of text;
> - **User Agent Control**: The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
> - **Essential**: A particular presentation of the target is essential to the information being conveyed.

Notes:
- **44 × 44 CSS px**, i.e. 44 CSS px per dimension — 20 CSS px larger per side than the AA minimum (44 − 24 = 20), and roughly 3.4× the area (1936 vs 576 CSS px²).
- 2.5.5 has **no spacing exception**; it relies on Equivalent / Inline / User Agent Control / Essential only. This is the key structural difference from 2.5.8.
- 2.5.5 was added in WCAG 2.1 (Level AAA) and is unchanged in 2.2; 2.5.8 was added in 2.2 (Level AA). They are cumulative in the usual WCAG sense: AAA conformance implies AA conformance.

## 5. Reference implementation

AA-conforming by size alone (no exception needed):

```css
/* 2.5.8 direct pass: 24 x 24 CSS px minimum hit area */
.icon-button {
  /* padding/border are part of the target box and count toward 24 x 24 */
  min-inline-size: 24px;
  min-block-size: 24px;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  /* margin is NOT part of the target and does NOT count toward size */
}
```

AAA-conforming by size alone:

```css
.icon-button {
  min-inline-size: 44px;
  min-block-size: 44px;
}
```

Smaller visual glyph, enlarged hit area (valid for AA; verify no overlap with neighbours):

```css
.chip {
  position: relative;          /* containing block for the hit area */
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}

.chip::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset-block-start: 50%;
  inset-inline-start: 50%;
  translate: -50% -50%;
  inline-size: max(100%, 24px);   /* 2.5.8: 24 CSS px */
  block-size: max(100%, 24px);
  /* do not let two ::after hit areas overlap, or the Spacing exception fails */
}
```

Dense icon row relying on the Spacing exception (20 px icons, 4 px gap → passes; 0 px gap → fails):

```css
.toolbar { display: flex; gap: 4px; }        /* 20 + 4 = 24 centre-to-centre: pass */
.toolbar--tight { display: flex; gap: 0; }   /* 20 + 0 = 20 centre-to-centre: fail  */
.toolbar .icon-button { inline-size: 20px; block-size: 20px; }
```

## 6. Audit procedure (repeatable)

1. Enumerate every pointer-operable target (links, buttons, inputs, custom widgets, `[role]`-interactive elements).
2. For each, take the bounding box of the actual hit region (element box + padding + border + any enlarged hit-area pseudo-element), in CSS px at 100% zoom.
3. If both dimensions ≥ 24 → **pass 2.5.8** (and note whether ≥ 44 for 2.5.5).
4. Else the target is undersized; test each exception in this order: Spacing, Equivalent, Inline, User Agent Control, Essential. Any one suffices.
5. Spacing test: draw a 24 CSS px diameter (12 px radius) circle centred on the target's bounding box. If it intersects no other target's bounding box and no other undersized target's circle → pass via Spacing.
6. For AAA, repeat with 44 × 44 and the four 2.5.5 exceptions; no spacing credit is available.
7. Re-check after text resizing/zoom, responsive reflow, and any state where labels, icons, or `line-height` change, since target boxes move.

## 7. Common misstatements this corrects

- "AA requires 44 × 44." — No. AA is 24 × 24 (2.5.8); 44 × 44 is AAA (2.5.5).
- "2.5.5 is the AA criterion." — No. 2.5.5 is Level AAA and predates 2.5.8.
- "The spacing circle is 24 px radius." — No. **24 px diameter**, 12 px radius.
- "The 24 px minimum always applies." — No. Five exceptions (Spacing, Equivalent, Inline, User Agent Control, Essential) can excuse a target below 24 × 24.
- "Margin counts toward target size." — No. Only the pointer-operable region counts.
